      "value": "workflow"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Implement a computational pipeline to test the NTK Rank Condition hypothesis by generating rigid and fat constraint families, training width-N networks to verify zero-violation capacity N_c(w), and computing NTK rank to validate the theoretical prediction. Steps: 1) Generate rigid family (constraints on low-dimensional manifold) and fat family (independent high-dimensional constraints); 2) Sweep N from 1 to w, train ReLU networks on w points, measure if max absolute error ≤ 1e-3 on held-out points; 3) Compute NTK matrix K(X,X) for each w, determine rank; 4) Compare empirical N_c(w) scaling (rigid: constant vs fat: linear) against NTK rank saturation (rigid: saturates at d vs fat: grows with w)."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "For rigid family: N_c(w) remains constant (~d) as w increases, and NTK rank saturates at d. For fat family: N_c(w) grows linearly with w, and NTK rank grows linearly with w until N ≈ w. The empirical N_c(w) should align with the NTK rank condition threshold."
    },
    {
